From: clasy...@gmail.com <Unknown>
Date: Monday, August 12, 2019 at 8:00:06 AM UTC-5
Subject: Problems resolving Windows Icon position in more recent releases 
of Windows
To: pepswin <Unknown>


I have posted screen shots of the main Commands window of the package; 
there have been some nice incremental changes as more features are added, 
etc.

It is abundantly clear that when you use a package with dozens of icons 
repeatedly as the most efficient and intuitive way to approach such a 
package (anyone else have any alternate ideas?) it became obvious you need 
a GUI where the user determines exactly what goes where on the screen, and 
everyone's approach is a bit different.

In Windows XP there was the useful feature of disabling the default 
behavior or auto-arranging and positioning in two different ways.

a) Ordering the icons merely by their alphanumeric order [or perhaps the 
exact inverse] is arbitrary and couinter-intuitive.  This can be defeated 
by a standard option of the view option in any window.  The setting has 
nothing to do with the desktop, rather every window gets an individual 
setting; clearly not all windows should be the same; the functionality 
dictates what is best and multiple situations call for multiple solutions, 
etc.  In Windows XP this was available from the beginning.

b) a nice frill was to snap the icon positions to an imaginary grid.  Most 
will agree that the rectilinear ordering as a separate option is a nice 
frill.

The problem is that the default was dumbed down so if you were lazy, you 
got something presentable.  Defaults can and should be capable of being 
over-ridden.

Not everyone is a fan of icons, it is of course a serving suggestion and 
there are in some Windows various sizes of icons.  But no one is likely  a 
fan of any mechanism where you lose control of the icons's position even in 
a list of say 40 or so icons on a screen window.  Clearly it can be tedious 
to keep having to mentally derive where something is in a list of detailed 
small icons.  Why?  Because the package is by nature DYNAMIC:

The release includes serving suggestions largely consisting of a pair of 
functions repeatedly applied to a different project file.  These are known 
as Edit *<project file name*> and Send  *<project file name*>  respectively.

The edit operation is quite intuitive.  You open the file in question in 
your editor of preference, which has rich options to determine exactly what 
you choose.  It includes a master editor choice determined by an 
environment variable and various one-off ways to override even that, if you 
wish to for whatever reason make this particular project file be treated 
differently.  The default is go with the environment variable; a setup icon 
in another setup-oriented window not normally on the screen sets which of 
the available compatible editors you wish; that list can grow as I find 
more and more editors that are suitable.  Not all are, and only recently 
have one or two been upgraded to be compatible enough, thus they have been 
added.  I suspect Ultra-Edit will also be compatible, but that is not 
available as freeware or shareware, so it remains a notion at this point, 
but clearly a future consideration; the scheme is flexible enough to add 
some cookie-cutter lines to all the relevant batch files for a newer 
release, etc.  I believe I can even get Anker Berg-Sonne's SEDT to be 
compatible, and already have added portable installation of Edit Pad or 
Edit Pad Lite freeware and NotePad++ with custom installation parameters 
primary overriding its defaults to make it easier to conform to the overall 
PEPS installation requirements.  [You may have Windows installed in any 
drive letter, and PEPS installed in its own drive, same or not, but the way 
Windows works, the default reference is to the drive PEPS is installed on.  
With a lot more work, this can be overcome to work with additional 
environment variables, so this would be a small "growing" pain; for the 
moment it is easier to insist on installing it in the same drive as PEPS.  
For most if you, everything will be on drive C: and for all of you, this is 
a non-consideration.  However, I RARELY if ever install Windows on drive C: 
for a variety of reasons.  Here's a big one:

Drive C: is the goto drive of choice of most malware.  The method of attack 
is to destroy some aspect of drive C:.  This is predictable.  Thus, the way 
I install Windows, drive C: is merely the bootup drive where none of my 
files are; there is just a small handful. nothing remotely to be called 
"data".  Should I get a serious attack, invariably it will destroy ALL of 
tiny drive C: and destroy the ability of the system to boot normally.

However, I also use Norton Ghost and that can be booted up separately, such 
as on diskettes or a flash drive.  From there I can easily RESTORE just 
drive C: from media as small as a diskette [or maybe a small data partition 
near the top of the drive or perhaps on a backup USB hard drive or on the 
flash drive for Ghost, etc. and in less than five minutes I am back up and 
running and ZERO data loss!  Note: There is a secondary issue a bit more 
esoteric.  Believing that drive C: is at least some imagined larger size, I 
also put important BUT BACKED UP data in drive D: which is bigger, but 
hardly ever is modified.  And of course it is also backed up.  Also, some 
of the attacks not only over-write that far from the beginning of the disk, 
they also destroy the disk partition structure.  That said, some older 
Norton Utilities can restore the partition table, and Ghost can certainly 
rebuild from backups.  Thus, for me drive C: is a terrible choice, and I 
recommend the minimum windows install drive to be at least drive E: and 
often I use drive G: or even higher.  The more you have that is 
"sacrificial' the better, but as part of the trade-off, in the unlikely 
event of partition table destruction, the more work to restore a hosted 
table.  Thus, my compromise is to implement Windows to at least drive E:.  
This is perhaps the choice that is best overall unless you are also a 
partition table expert like me, in which case go even higher.  Regardless 
of these secondary issues, use drive C: at your own risk!  And even if you 
have a brand-name system, there are ways to find out your legitimate 
product key, and then use a freeware partitioning program such as Easus 
Partition Master to partition your disk any way you wish, the use a good 
backup/restore program like Ghost or Acronis to maintain the more 
intelligent set of partitions.  This violates nothing whatsoever regarding 
your machine, unless you believe their customer-service monkeys are a 
"valuable resource".  I tend to think PDP-8 users are better than that 
anyway!

In any case,PEPS forces no Windows  drive, you choose it, but it does need 
to know which is the boot drive, and uses environment variables of Windows 
to determine this, etc.  By a little bit of future work, I can add the 
ability to overcome these modest drive overrides when you install these 
editors.  Clearly changing a few characters in an "advanced" installation 
optional screen is no big deal, so this is actually a very low priority 
aspect of PEPS, etc.  More important is to get more choices, not less 
easy-still choices.

There are two main ways to use one if these Edit icon-based commands per 
project tile:

1) Edir the file and exit in the conventional sense.  Easiest but not 
likely the most efficient.  When the Edit command exits, it is now off the 
screen and the file is copied to the internal file that represents the 
paper-tape input file passed to SIMH.  The next step is you run either OS/8 
or P?S/8 from the main command section of the Commands Window.  P?S/8 has 
many variants possible:

a) P?S/8 with full LPT: support.  This is what most people would use to run 
P?S/8.  Unless over-ridden, the output that MIGHT go to the console ALWAYS 
goes to the LPT:  Most of not all programs include such as a /T switch to 
override this.  I have not checked all current conforming programs, but it 
is clear some years ago, NOT all programs had this capability, but clearly 
most of them do.

Note: Some but not all programs also support an internal feature to XOR a 
mask of 36 bits against the command-line switches passed to the program.  
What this means is that if you set the corresponding bit in the mask for /T 
the meaning of /T is reversed.  Thus, you could then make /T mean CHECK FOR 
THE LPT:  But not all programs support this feature, and to patch it 
requires specific documentation.  In theory, a future release could 
aggregate the information to make a central patching feature, but this 
would be a very low priority for P?S/8.  A more important frill would be to 
implement all conforming programs to support the feature at all; this is a 
matter of additional once-only initialization code patterned after the few 
programs that already have it.  The goal would be to not make the program 
images longer by a block, but with some effort, this should be the case.  
This is on the laundry list for the next complete release of P?S/8, etc.

b) P?S/8 with NO LPT: support.  This makes the /T issue moot and works for 
all programs including the ones that either don't or can't use the LPT: or 
don't yet have implemented the /T switch at all.  A nearer term goal for 
the next complete release is to make sure ALL such programs DO support the 
/T switch.  I believe at this point there are slim to none of them, pending 
further research, but I am 100% sure no program NEEDS the /T switch for an 
alternate purpose.

c) Some users want to see P?S/8 Version 8T instead of the current 
patched-up version 8Z.  This is more for historic purposes than anything 
else:

The original system P?S/8 is based on is the original R-L Monitor a less 
capable system which was essentially the proof-of-concept forerunner which 
was written by Richard Lary before he was a DEC employee and coerced by DEC 
management to create half-baked OS/8's original mediocrity.  Please note 
that the members of the team all quit over this because this was a demented 
code freeze to a demo version hacked and cobbled up in less than 48 hours 
over a weekend just to give them something to look at while everyone else 
was designing and brainstorming new features and a major redesign.  As 
such, the code-frozen OS/8 is half-baked and incomplete and unfixable.  
This is why the function word low-order three bits are unused; they were 
INTENDED to be the same as is in P?S/8, the logical unit bit of a device if 
relevant.  This is why OS/8 needs EIGHT separate handlers for eight DECtape 
drives on a TC01/TC08 [which is supported by PEPS in SIMH) instead of a DTA 
handler with logical unit calls to one handler. [And that in turn weakens 
OS/8 since you can have only 14 unique handlers because it couldn't add the 
ability to have either 15 or 16 due to the need to rewrite sections that 
were poor in the frozen code.  The concept of DSK: in OS/8 is a dummy, but 
internally counts as if a real device.  A better design would put the 
logical device number somewhere else to mean what should you use the syntax 
DSK: is to mean, that gets you 15.  And by having alternate means to 
delineate the table, you can avoid the need for an internal sentinel.But 
this is what you get when start to finish the demo hack is created in 48 
hours!  The rational thought of expert programmers not under an artificial 
deadline was insultingly ignored, and the "expertise" of dimwitted managers 
override their own staff!  As a result the other four literally walked out 
on the project.  I personally was asked to join, but I refused for two 
reasons: a) it would require relocation which I would never do;  I like the 
area as a place to visit, but not to live, b) I told Richard Lary that I 
and others were improving the R-L Monitor [today the improved do-over is 
exactly what P?S/8 actually is all about].  While disappointed, he couldn't 
fault my decision to work on an open-ended improvement to a good start 
instead of being a slave to a permanent mediocrity that could never be 
fixed [and indeed it never was!].  Note:I was tipped off about this by one 
of the other four who walked out, etc.

P?S/8 Version 8T uses the original R-L commands and has literally over a 
decade less work invested in it,so all of the programs are either less 
capable or totally lacking.  And very few if any have a /T switch concept, 
so it is mostly necessary to have a version with LPT: support disabled.  As 
such, there are four different P?S/8 configurations and one OS/8 
configuration to allow all variants.

Note: The explanation is a bit murky, but  needs a bit of explanation:

DEC sold a product called "Dibol-8" which was a thinly disguised hacked-up 
R-L Monitor system.  The only changes are a) The first implementation of a 
DIBOL compiler and run-time system was added, b) Some frill programs were 
removed, c) most of the commands that were to be changed in the future were 
hacked in so that future releases would be familiar to users upgrading.

All of these systems are approached in a manner like typical BASIC 
implementations, and most of the people mentioned here are actually BASIC 
language experts..  They wanted to create a system that worked like BASIC 
and the commands were intuitively very much like BASIC including commands 
to LOad files and SAve files,etc.

However, in future systems, these commands would become reserved for 
alternate functions that help to implement other capabilities in a concise 
command form  Independently of this, P?S/8 also took a similar 
implementation route.  As such, some of the two-letter command names had to 
be changed to allow longer names for the new functions.  As such, it was 
necessary to change the exact command names; this largely applied to 
DIBOL-8's hacked command table as well.

Thus, eventually P?S/8 more resembles [superficially, this is only 
addressing the NAMES of the commands and doesn't imply much more] the 
DIBOL-8 successor COS-300/310.

Here are some relevant examples:

a) The Load command became the FEtch command.  Sorta the same intuition but 
different.

b) The SAve command became the WRite command.  Ditto for that reasoning.

c) Autosequencing the system line editor was originally done by the 
SEquence command.  This was changed to the LNumber command; not quite as 
intuitive, but it was important to have a SET command.

In P?S/8 Version 8T, there is a utility to set various keyboard monitor 
parameters.  It is known as VARY *<option>* to be non-conflicting.  In 
P?S/8 Version 8Z it was changed to the more intuitive SET *<option>*  
command utility.

d) In borh R-L and early P?S/8 and also DIBOL and COS-300/310, there is a 
feature to pause a batch operation and wait for an any keypress.  This was 
implemented as such:

PAuse. PLEASE ENABLE THE LINEPRINTER

This prints out the entire line even if echo off is in effect.  Waits for a 
key and then continues.

All of them changed this to:

PLease ENABLE THE LINEPRINTER [Note: in all examples, the lower-case 
represents typical optional characters]

P?S/8 added the comment comment thus, you might see after the keypress:

^^THANK YOU!

This in turn frees up the PAL command to run the P?S/8 PAL assembler, and 
in OS/8 it is the same identical command form [but the options are 
different specific meaning and there are more of them].

But both COS and P?S/8 do not use overhead-ridden CCL, just that the 
command language is already concise inherently.  [Note: Newer DEC systems 
also abandoned CCL due to the overhead.  Eventually, DEC;s official 
position was, due to the inherent overhead, it is NOT RECOMMEND to enable 
CCL on tape-based systems.  Note: If the system device is a DF32, this is 
moot!  There isn't enough storage space to implement CCL !

Thus, the two variant P/S/8 Version 8T systems are not of concern for most 
users, but are supported internally within the PEPS package/  The icons for 
the two relevant commands are always available if needed.

Generally, across all P?S/8 implementations the rest of the commands 
haven't changed merely because they are non-conflicting.  We don't change 
things only for the sake of changing things!



