ck is created in 48 hours!&nbsp; The rational thought of expert programmers=
 not under an artificial deadline was insultingly ignored, and the "experti=
se" of dimwitted managers override their own staff!&nbsp; As a result the o=
ther four literally walked out on the project.&nbsp; I personally was asked=
 to join, but I refused for two reasons: a) it would require relocation whi=
ch I would never do;&nbsp; I like the area as a place to visit, but not to =
live, b) I told Richard Lary that I and others were improving the R-L Monit=
or [today the improved do-over is exactly what P?S/8 actually is all about]=
.&nbsp; While disappointed, he couldn't fault my decision to work on an ope=
n-ended improvement to a good start instead of being a slave to a permanent=
 mediocrity that could never be fixed [and indeed it never was!].&nbsp; Not=
e:I was tipped off about this by one of the other four who walked out, etc.=
</div><div><br></div><div>P?S/8 Version 8T uses the original R-L commands a=
nd has literally over a decade less work invested in it,so all of the progr=
ams are either less capable or totally lacking.&nbsp; And very few if any h=
ave a /T switch concept, so it is mostly necessary to have a version with L=
PT: support disabled.&nbsp; As such, there are four different P?S/8 configu=
rations and one OS/8 configuration to allow all variants.<br><br>Note: The =
explanation is a bit murky, but&nbsp; needs a bit of explanation:</div><div=
><br></div><div>DEC sold a product called "Dibol-8" which was a thinly disg=
uised hacked-up R-L Monitor system.&nbsp; The only changes are a) The first=
 implementation of a DIBOL compiler and run-time system was added, b) Some =
frill programs were removed, c) most of the commands that were to be change=
d in the future were hacked in so that future releases would be familiar to=
 users upgrading.</div><div><br></div><div>All of these systems are approac=
hed in a manner like typical BASIC implementations, and most of the people =
mentioned here are actually BASIC language experts..&nbsp; They wanted to c=
reate a system that worked like BASIC and the commands were intuitively ver=
y much like BASIC including commands to LOad files and SAve files,etc.<br><=
br>However, in future systems, these commands would become reserved for alt=
ernate functions that help to implement other capabilities in a concise com=
mand form&nbsp; Independently of this, P?S/8 also took a similar implementa=
tion route.&nbsp; As such, some of the two-letter command names had to be c=
hanged to allow longer names for the new functions.&nbsp; As such, it was n=
ecessary to change the exact command names; this largely applied to DIBOL-8=
's hacked command table as well.</div><div><br></div><div>Thus, eventually =
P?S/8 more resembles [superficially, this is only addressing the NAMES of t=
he commands and doesn't imply much more] the DIBOL-8 successor COS-300/310.=
</div><div><br></div><div>Here are some relevant examples:</div><div><br></=
div><div>a) The Load command became the FEtch command.&nbsp; Sorta the same=
 intuition but different.</div><div><br></div><div>b) The SAve command beca=
me the WRite command.&nbsp; Ditto for that reasoning.</div><div><br></div><=
div>c) Autosequencing the system line editor was originally done by the SEq=
uence command.&nbsp; This was changed to the LNumber command; not quite as =
intuitive, but it was important to have a SET command.</div><div><br></div>=
<div>In P?S/8 Version 8T, there is a utility to set various keyboard monito=
r parameters.&nbsp; It is known as VARY <i>&lt;option&gt;</i>&nbsp;to be no=
n-conflicting.&nbsp; In P?S/8 Version 8Z it was changed to the more intuiti=
ve SET&nbsp;<i>&lt;option&gt;</i>&nbsp; command utility.</div><div><br></di=
v><div>d) In borh R-L and early P?S/8 and also DIBOL and COS-300/310, there=
 is a feature to pause a batch operation and wait for an any keypress.&nbsp=
; This was implemented as such:</div><div><br></div><div>PAuse. PLEASE ENAB=
LE THE LINEPRINTER</div><div><br></div><div>This prints out the entire line=
 even if echo off is in effect.&nbsp; Waits for a key and then continues.</=
div><div><br></div><div>All of them changed this to:</div><div><br></div><d=
iv>PLease ENABLE THE LINEPRINTER [Note: in all examples, the lower-case rep=
resents typical optional characters]</div><div><br></div><div>P?S/8 added t=
he comment comment thus, you might see after the keypress:</div><div><br></=
div><div>^^THANK YOU!</div><div><br></div><div>This in turn frees up the PA=
L command to run the P?S/8 PAL assembler, and in OS/8 it is the same identi=
cal command form [but the options are different specific meaning and there =
are more of them].</div><div><br></div><div>But both COS and P?S/8 do not u=
se overhead-ridden CCL, just that the command language is already concise i=
nherently.&nbsp; [Note: Newer DEC systems also abandoned CCL due to the ove=
rhead.&nbsp; Eventually, DEC;s official position was, due to the inherent o=
verhead, it is NOT RECOMMEND to enable CCL on tape-based systems.&nbsp; Not=
e: If the system device is a DF32, this is moot!&nbsp; There isn't enough s=
torage space to implement CCL !</div><div><br></div><div>Thus, the two vari=
ant P/S/8 Version 8T systems are not of concern for most users, but are sup=
ported internally within the PEPS package/&nbsp; The icons for the two rele=
vant commands are always available if needed.</div><div><br></div><div>Gene=
rally, across all P?S/8 implementations the rest of the commands haven't ch=
anged merely because they are non-conflicting.&nbsp; We don't change things=
 only for the sake of changing things!</div><div><br><br></div></div></html=
